  data:
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'DISASSEMBLING AND ASSEMBLING A COMPUTER

'
    description: "Introduction\r\n \r\nYou might be wondering why the lesson  starts
      with disassembly and not assembly itself? Simply because we want to learn how
      to disassemble so we can assemble it again from how we took out the parts.\r\n
      \r\n \r\nIt is because in the industry, you will be performing disassembly
      especially in servicing, compared to building a new computer and repairing
      and maintaining the computers in the workplace. You will have to be familiar
      especially first with the system unit. We want you to learn how to assemble
      what you've taken out (disassembled).\r\n \r\n \r\nIt is important that you
      have taken and reviewed the prerequisite module (Intro to CSS) before going
      into this hands-on MODULE.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 8256683e3102f354f9f6a4352d4e6faa, type: 3}
    titleName: 'Preparing for Disassembly

'
    description: 'Before going into the disassembly proper, you will have to prepare
      all the materials that you will need.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Computer Disassembly Procedure

'
    description: "a.  Gather all the necessary tools first on the work area.\r\nyou
      will be needing the following tools:\r\n1. Screwdriver\r\n2. Flashligth\r\n3.Electrostatic
      Mat\r\n4. Anti Static Wrist Wrap\r\n5. Thermal Paste\r\nb. Unplug the device
      from the wall or power source.\r\nc. first, place the electrotastic mat on
      the work place\r\nd. Wrap or attach an anti-static wrist strap and connect
      it to an unpainted metal surface. This will prevent static electricity from
      damaging the components.\r\n \r\ne. Open the case: Remove the side panel from
      the computer case. (Note: The exact method might look different depending on
      your case style). Make sure to check the system case for all installed devices.\r\n
      \r\nf. Remove all internal power connections inside, starting with the main
      power connectors (such as the 24-pin volts power connector) and any Molex or
      SATA power cables.\r\ng. To remove the power supply from the case, hold the
      power supply securely. Unscrew the mounting screws holding the power supply
      and carefully pull it out. (Keep track of the screws so you won't misplace
      them).\r\nh. Remove the drive bays / optical drives, Look at the upper portion
      of the case. Remove the bolts/screws securing the drive to the system case.
      Slide the drive out to remove it.\r\n \r\ni. Remove the hard disk drive (HDD/SSD),
      Locate the drive based at the bottom or middle sections. Unscrew the hard disk
      from the bay. Slide the drive out to remove it.\r\n \r\nj.  Remove the bolts/screws
      that secure your motherboard to the system case.\r\nK. Remove the RAM (Memory
      Modules), locate the RAM slots on the motherboard.\r\npush down the tabs or
      clips on both ends of the slots. slowly pull out the memory stick once it is
      released from its place.\r\n \r\nL Remove the CPU Fan & Processor, detach the
      CPU fan from its mounting on the motherboard. Lift the metal retention lever
      on the CPU socket that holds the processor. Look closely at the arrow indicators
      on the processor and the socket. Take care when lifting the processor out of
      the socket.\r\n \r\nm. Final steps: Once you've safely removed these components,
      document the state of each component for storage or cleaning.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 26a98c76641dda14199b8c581c51901e, type: 3}
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 4ab2aea5d4852694385b4b0546dc633c, type: 3}
    titleName: 'Performance Checklist

'
    description: "If you tried disassembling a computer on your own, this checklist
      might be able to help you if you've done the job correctly.\r\n \r\nCheck the
      following if you've met the criteria:\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 24db688d7cfd19b43bd85ce50fb6a1e0, type: 3}
    titleName: 'Assembling a Computer

'
    description: "Introduction\r\n \r\n \r\nNow that you've learned how to disassemble
      a PC, we are now going to proceed to assembling what we just disassembled.
      Ideally, as seen in the last part of the disassembly video, you need to document
      the specifications/status of the components before putting back the system
      unit together again.\r\n \r\nA helpful tip is since you worked your way part
      by part in disassembly, now you are just going to retrace your steps in assembling
      it.\r\n \r\nBefore going into the assembly proper, you will have to prepare
      all the materials that you will need.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 24db688d7cfd19b43bd85ce50fb6a1e0, type: 3}
    titleName: 'Computer Assembly Procedure

'
    description: "a.  Gather all the necessary tools first on the work area.\r\nyou
      will be needing the following tools:\r\n1. Screwdriver\r\n2. Flashligth\r\n3.Electrostatic
      Mat\r\n4. Anti Static Wrist Wrap\r\n5. Thermal Paste\r\n \r\nb. first, place
      the electrotastic mat on the work place\r\nc. Wrap or attach an anti-static
      wrist strap and connect it to an unpainted metal surface. This will prevent
      static electricity from damaging the components.\r\nd. Remove the side panels
      and lift them away from the computer case. (Check the case manual first, as
      different cases open differently).\r\n \r\ne. Check the brand-specific information
      for your processor before installing.\r\nf. Clean the CPU fan and the heatsink
      with a brush.\r\ng. Lift the metal load rod next to the CPU socket.Find the
      alignment mark on the corner of the CPU and carefully insert it into the socket,
      lining up the markings.\r\nH. Push the metal rod back down to lock the processor
      securely into place.\r\ni. Following the instructions provided, place a small
      amount of thermal compound onto the CPU and the heatsink.\r\n \r\nj. Align
      the CPU fan on the mounting tabs over the socket. Screw the fan to the motherboard
      and pull down its locking rod to secure it.\r\nk. : Connect the fan's power
      cable to the CPU fan header on the motherboard.\r\nl. Locate the memory slots
      on the motherboard. Align the RAM stick notches with the slot and gently push
      down from both ends until the tabs click and lock the RAM into place.\r\n \r\nm.
      Screw the standoffs into the side of the system case. Align the motherboard's
      ports with the I/O bezel, place it onto the standoffs, and screw it down tightly.\r\n
      \r\nn. Locate the hard drive bays in the system case. Slide the hard disk into
      the bay until the screw holes on both the drive and the case line up, then
      screw them tightly.\r\n \r\no. \u2022 \tLocate the drive bay usually found
      on the upper portion of the case. From the outside, slide the optical drive
      in until the screw holes align, then screw it tightly into place.\r\n \r\np.
      \u2022\tMount the power supply into its designated large box area in the case
      (placement varies by brand). Align the screw holes of the PSU with the case
      and screw them tightly.\r\n \r\nq. Connect all front panel connectors. Carefully
      read your case and motherboard manuals for proper cable placement instructions.\r\n
      \r\nr. Connect all power supply cables to their respective devices and motherboard
      headers. Carefully read your case and motherboard manuals for proper cable
      placement instructions.\r\n \r\n \r\ns.  Attach the system unit to a monitor,
      keyboard, and mouse.\r\n      \ti. Installing Keyboard\r\n               \t\r\n\xB7        
      Make sure to read the instruction manual of your keyboard first.\r\n\xB7        
      Check whether the keyboard has a USB (flat, rectangular shape) or PS/2 (typically
      round and purple colored) connector.\r\n \r\n\xB7         Install the keyboard
      by connecting it to its proper port.\r\n\xB7         Do not install the keyboard
      while the computer is powered on. Do not remove the keyboard while the computer
      is running.\r\n \r\n      \tii. Mouse\r\n\xB7         The process to installing
      this device is similar to that of the keyboard. Check whether your mouse is
      using a PS/2 Connector or a USB connector then connect it to its proper port.\r\n\xB7        
      For bluetooth mouse, kindly follow the manufacturer manual\r\n \r\n      \tiii.
      Monitor\r\n\xB7         Identify the connection type that will connect the
      monitor to the  motherboard or the graphics card.\r\n\xB7         A DVI, VGA,
      or HDMI are common ports in the monitor and  used  to produce best picture
      qualities.\r\n\xB7         Once the display is plugged to its proper connector
      on the video card or the motherboard, the display is ready. Most of the time,
      drivers are not required but it should be downloaded to utilize a maximum resolution.\r\n
      \r\n      \tiv. Speakers\r\n\xB7         Determine the type of speaker and
      its connector.\r\n\xB7         Connect the cable (usually a 3.5mm jack) to
      the line-out jack of the computer sound card or on the system unit.\r\n\xB7        
      Plug in the power cord.\r\n \r\n      \tv. Webcam\r\n\xB7         Common webcams
      can be simply plugged into the USB port and you will be prompted to install
      their drivers. Some webcams would need to have its driver installed first.
      Make sure to read the manufacturer\u2019s instruction manual first.\r\n \r\n     
      \tvi. Printer\r\n\xB7         Make sure to have the printer and power cable.\r\n\xB7        
      Connect the printer connector to the unit.\r\n\xB7         Insert the ink or
      toner cartridge to the printer.\r\n\xB7         Turn the printer on.\r\n\xB7        
      Power the computer.\r\n\xB7         Start the Printer Install Wizard.\r\n\xB7        
      Follow the instructions to install the Printer Driver.\r\n\xB7         Run
      a test print to check if the printer isinstalled correctly.\r\n \r\nt.   Turn
      on the computer and press the designated key to run the BIOS setup (usually
      Delete, F2, or F12). Verify that all connected devices are visible in the BIOS.
      Once verified, you have successfully assembled the computer and can proceed
      to configure the BIOS and install the operating system.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
